{"duration_api_ms":39235,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"fab102ba-ef90-489c-9fb9-42cb2832ca59","total_cost_usd":0.1897958,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11032,"cache_read_input_tokens":1459,"output_tokens":5062,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":2004},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11032,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":5062,"cache_read_input_tokens":1459,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11032,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11032},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-5-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":5062,"cacheReadInputTokens":1459,"cacheCreationInputTokens":11032,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.1897958,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":2004,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"merged_document\":\"# BIP: 39\\n\\n## Abstract\\n\\nThis BIP describes the implementation of a mnemonic code or mnemonic sentence -- a group of easy to remember words -- for the generation of deterministic wallets.\\n\\nIt consists of two parts: generating the mnemonic and converting it into a binary seed. This seed can be later used to generate deterministic wallets using BIP-0032 or similar methods.\\n\\n## Copyright\\n\\nThis BIP is licensed under the 2-clause BSD license.\\n\\n## Motivation\\n\\nA mnemonic code or sentence is superior for human interaction compared to the handling of raw binary or hexadecimal representations of a wallet seed. The sentence could be written on paper or spoken over the telephone.\\n\\nThis guide is meant to be a way to transport computer-generated randomness with a human-readable transcription. It's not a way to process user-created sentences (also known as brainwallets) into a wallet seed.\\n\\n## Generating the mnemonic\\n\\nThe mnemonic must encode entropy in a multiple of 32 bits. With more entropy security is improved but the sentence length increases. We refer to the initial entropy length as ENT. The allowed size of ENT is 128-256 bits.\\n\\nFirst, an initial entropy of ENT bits is generated. A checksum is generated by taking the first ENT / 32 bits of its SHA256 hash. This checksum is appended to the end of the initial entropy. Next, these concatenated bits are split into groups of 11 bits, each encoding a number from 0-2047, serving as an index into a wordlist. Finally, we convert these numbers into words and use the joined words as a mnemonic sentence.\\n\\nThe following table describes the relation between the initial entropy length (ENT), the checksum length (CS), and the length of the generated mnemonic sentence (MS) in words.\\n\\n```\\nCS = ENT / 32\\nMS = (ENT + CS) / 11\\n\\n|  ENT  | CS | ENT+CS |  MS  |\\n+-------+----+--------+------+\\n|  128  |  4 |   132  |  12  |\\n|  160  |  5 |   165  |  15  |\\n|  192  |  6 |   198  |  18  |\\n|  224  |  7 |   231  |  21  |\\n|  256  |  8 |   264  |  24  |\\n```\\n\\nSource: https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0039.mediawiki\",\"decisions\":[{\"slot\":\"title\",\"candidates\":[{\"text\":\"# BIP: 39\",\"document\":\"source_a.md\"}],\"chosen\":\"# BIP: 39\",\"reason\":\"Base document's title; source_b.md carries only a section heading.\"},{\"slot\":\"placement of mnemonic generation section\",\"candidates\":[{\"text\":\"## Generating the memonic\",\"document\":\"source_b.md\"}],\"chosen\":\"## Generating the mnemonic\",\"reason\":\"Base has no such section; appended after Motivation to keep base order.\"}],\"dispositions\":[{\"segment\":\"a3\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"This BIP describes the implementation of a mnemonic code or mnemonic sentence -- a group of easy to remember words -- for the generation of deterministic wallets.\",\"reason\":\"Abstract: corrected 'letters' to words and 'UTOXs' to wallets.\"},{\"segment\":\"a4\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"It consists of two parts: generating the mnemonic and converting it into a binary seed.\",\"reason\":\"Abstract: BIP 39 produces a binary seed, not an ASCII seed.\"},{\"segment\":\"a7\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"This BIP is licensed under the 2-clause BSD license.\",\"reason\":\"Copyright: BIP 39 is 2-clause BSD licensed, not GPL.\"},{\"segment\":\"a9\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"A mnemonic code or sentence is superior for human interaction compared to the handling of raw binary or hexadecimal representations of a wallet seed.\",\"reason\":\"Motivation: the representation is of a wallet seed, not satoshis.\"},{\"segment\":\"a11\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"This guide is meant to be a way to transport computer-generated randomness with a human-readable transcription.\",\"reason\":\"Motivation: the mnemonic transports randomness, not determinism.\"},{\"segment\":\"a12\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"It's not a way to process user-created sentences (also known as brainwallets) into a wallet seed.\",\"reason\":\"Motivation: fixed misspelling 'süeed'.\"},{\"segment\":\"b1\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"## Generating the mnemonic\",\"reason\":\"Section heading: fixed misspelling 'memonic'.\"},{\"segment\":\"b2\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"The mnemonic must encode entropy in a multiple of 32 bits.\",\"reason\":\"Fixed 'demonic' typo; entropy is a multiple of 32 bits, not 23.\"},{\"segment\":\"b5\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"The allowed size of ENT is 128-256 bits.\",\"reason\":\"ENT range is 128-256 bits, matching the table; fixed 'byts'.\"},{\"segment\":\"b7\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"A checksum is generated by taking the first ENT / 32 bits of its SHA256 hash.\",\"reason\":\"Checksum uses SHA256; 'SHA652' does not exist.\"},{\"segment\":\"b8\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"This checksum is appended to the end of the initial entropy.\",\"reason\":\"Checksum is appended to the end of the entropy, not the start.\"},{\"segment\":\"b9\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"Next, these concatenated bits are split into groups of 11 bits, each encoding a number from 0-2047, serving as an index into a wordlist.\",\"reason\":\"Groups are 11 bits (0-2047), matching MS = (ENT + CS) / 11.\"}],\"mismatch\":\"\",\"additions\":[{\"statement\":\"This BIP describes the implementation of a mnemonic code or mnemonic sentence -- a group of easy to remember words -- for the generation of deterministic wallets.\",\"corrects\":\"a group of easy to remember letters -- for the generation of deterministic UTOXs\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"BIP 39 mnemonics are words and generate deterministic wallets.\"},{\"statement\":\"It consists of two parts: generating the mnemonic and converting it into a binary seed.\",\"corrects\":\"converting it into a ASCII seed\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"BIP 39 derives a 512-bit binary seed via PBKDF2.\"},{\"statement\":\"This BIP is licensed under the 2-clause BSD license.\",\"corrects\":\"This BIP falls under the GPL License.\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"BIP 39's Copyright section states the 2-clause BSD license.\"},{\"statement\":\"A mnemonic code or sentence is superior for human interaction compared to the handling of raw binary or hexadecimal representations of a wallet seed.\",\"corrects\":\"representations of a wallet satoshis\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"BIP 39 Motivation refers to representations of a wallet seed.\"},{\"statement\":\"This guide is meant to be a way to transport computer-generated randomness with a human-readable transcription.\",\"corrects\":\"computer-generated determinism\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"BIP 39 Motivation says computer-generated randomness.\"},{\"statement\":\"The mnemonic must encode entropy in a multiple of 32 bits.\",\"corrects\":\"a multiple of 23 bits\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"BIP 39 requires 32-bit multiples; CS = ENT / 32 in the table.\"},{\"statement\":\"The allowed size of ENT is 128-256 bits.\",\"corrects\":\"The allowed size of ENT is 1024-2048 byts.\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"BIP 39 allows 128-256 bits, as the document's own table shows.\"},{\"statement\":\"A checksum is generated by taking the first ENT / 32 bits of its SHA256 hash.\",\"corrects\":\"SHA652 hash\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"BIP 39 checksum uses SHA256; no SHA652 exists.\"},{\"statement\":\"This checksum is appended to the end of the initial entropy.\",\"corrects\":\"appended to the start of the initial entropy\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"BIP 39 appends the checksum to the end of the entropy.\"},{\"statement\":\"Next, these concatenated bits are split into groups of 11 bits, each encoding a number from 0-2047, serving as an index into a wordlist.\",\"corrects\":\"groups of 12 bits\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"11 bits encode 0-2047 and match MS = (ENT + CS) / 11.\"}]}","ttft_ms":23830,"type":"result","duration_ms":43759,"uuid":"0d77d30a-66fb-4c32-a69e-22740f7bb01b","ttft_stream_ms":5810,"time_to_request_ms":4521,"first_content_frame_ms":5810,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
